5.2   Maturity Assessment of Ecosystems
    In the context of ecosystem development, a gradual transition is observed, accompanied by
alterations in species richness, biomass (total bioenergy), P/R ratio, energy utilization, and food
web complexity.
    In order to assess the stability and maturity changes of the previously mentioned wheat field
ecosystem (transformed by forested areas), the maturity index of the ecosystem, ΩEcoMat , was de-
fined and the meaning of the indicators in it was explained as follows,

                              Table 4: Maturity Index of Ecosystem
             Index                              Description
              SR         Species Richness, measures the stability of the ecosystem
              BM        Biomass, measures the development level of the ecosystem
              P/R              Ratio of total production to total respiration
             EUIR      Energy Utilization Rate, specifically photosynthetic efficiency
             FWC                          Food Web Complexity

    The maturity index is derived from the weights of the aforementioned indicators, which are
evaluated by experts. However, it should be noted that the weights employed in this process are
exclusively utilized to regulate the order of magnitude consistency, as demonstrated by the follow-
ing formula:

                                                       P
                 ΩEcoMat = ω1 · S R + ω2 · BM + ω3 ·     + ω4 · EUR + ω5 · FWC                        (14)
                                                       R
   Calculate the Maturity Index of the Wheat Food Web model and draw a graph of its variation
over time, as well as graphs of the different metrics, as shown in Figure 8.




            (a) Photosynthetic Efficiency                   (b) Maturity Index of Ecosystem

